# 01 · The workload: DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash on deepseek-ultrafast

All numbers on this page come from the code. The inputs are the released model files and
`basetenlabs/deepseek-ultrafast` @ `{{ REP.source.commit[:8] }}`. The outputs are from
`experiments/workload_analysis.py` → `results/workload.json`. We quote the deepseek-ultrafast
measurements. We did not reproduce them. The rest is **modeled**.

## 1. What the engine runs (read from the code, not the README)

The repository serves DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash (not V3/R1) on Blackwell Ultra:

| item | value | where it comes from |
|---|---|---|
| model | **DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash**: 40-layer Causal Encoder-Decoder (20 + 20), multimodal, {{ (w.param_counts.backbone_total/1e9)|n(0) }}B backbone + {{ (w.param_counts.engram_tables/1e9)|n(1) }}B Engram parameters | `crates/model/src/config.rs`, HF `config.json` |
| hardware | 4 x GB300 (TP4) or 8 x B300 (TP8), `sm_103a` | README |
| batch | **one request at a time**: the server holds one `Job` that each rank executes | `crates/server/src/engine.rs` (`Shared.job`, `next_job`) |
| parallelism | pure tensor parallelism. The engine shards query heads, `wo_a` groups, `wo_b` rows, the vocab, **and each routed/shared expert by its intermediate dim** (2304/TP). The engine **replicates** `wq_a`+`wkv`, the indexer `wq_b`, compressor, gate and mHC projections. There is no expert parallelism and no all-to-all. Each layer has two all-reduces, fused with the mHC epilogue (NVLS / Lamport buffers) | `crates/engine/src/weights.rs::layer`, `model.rs::comm_ex`, `cu/comm.cu` |
| speculation | DSpark: 3 drafter blocks (SWA-only attention, 128-expert top-3 MoE) draft 5 tokens. The backbone verifies **6 rows** per step (adaptive length through a CUDA-graph conditional switch) | `model.rs::capture_spec_blk`, `capture_spec_vadapt` |
| weights | routed experts **FP4 E2M1** with an E8M0 scale per 32 (stored as packed `I8`), dense projections **FP8 E4M3** with E8M0 per 32x32 block, head **BF16** (the engine uses an FP8 copy for the draft, and for verify on GB300 TP4). In memory, the engine pads the slice of each rank to 128 rows. But the routed-expert tensor maps read only the real rows plus the 64-row padding of w2: **+{{ ((W.layout_factor.tp4-1)*100)|n(1) }}% streamed routed-expert bytes at TP4, +{{ ((W.layout_factor.tp8-1)*100)|n(1) }}% at TP8**. The engine reads the FP8 copy of the shared expert with its padding | safetensors headers (`data/checkpoint_manifest.csv`), `ops.rs::verify_head_fp8`, `packing.rs::sgl_expert_bytes`, `model.rs::sgl_init` |
| KV cache | main (compressed) KV **FP4** E2M1 + E4M3 scale per 16 ch; SWA KV FP8; indexer K FP4 | tech report §2.4.4, `inference/model.py` |
| kernels | hand-written CUDA for tcgen05: weight-streaming GEMV (`tcgemv*`), fused MoE (`tcmoe`, `sgl`), sparse attention, top-k, Mega-mHC (`hcpipe`), Markov draft head, Gumbel sampler. Each launch uses programmatic dependent launch (PDL). The indexer, compressor and mHC dot products run on side streams. The producer kernel pushes the all-reduces (NVLS multimem + Lamport buffers). Our op graph counts about {{ w.kernels_per_step }} dependent kernel stages per step on the critical path (approximate) | `crates/kernels/cu/*.cu`, `model.rs` (`launch`, `par_fork`, `comm_ex`) |

