The last row is the one discrepancy. Figure 2 of the paper reports +25% decode FLOPs from 4K to 1M
context. Our op count gives +{{ (w.validation[-1].ours*100)|n(0) }}%. Almost all of the growth comes
from indexer scoring. The layer-20 Full indexer scans the full context, and the three
encoder Full indexers scan half of it.

We attribute the extra growth to a difference in convention
(for example, whether the count includes the index-score reduction). The discrepancy has no effect at the measured
~2.6K context, where the indexer is less than 1% of a step.

## 3. Decode: FLOPs and bytes per output token

One step drafts 5 tokens, verifies 6 rows through all 40 layers and emits {{ w.decode_step.accept_len }} tokens on
average. With independent routing, the 6 verify rows touch **{{ w.decode_step.distinct_experts_verify|n(1) }}
distinct experts per layer**. The drafter touches {{ w.decode_step.distinct_experts_draft|n(1) }} per block. One step reads
**{{ (w.decode_step.unique_bytes/1e9)|n(1) }} GB** of unique bytes and does {{ (w.decode_step.flops/1e9)|n(0) }} GFLOP.
The table shows these values per emitted token (system-wide, batch 1, context 2.6K):

| op class | FLOPs / token | HBM bytes / token | FLOP/B | bound on B300 (FP8 ridge {{ w.ridge.fp8|n(0) }} FLOP/B) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
