Notes:
- **Routed experts dominate.** They are {{ pct(w.decode_table|selectattr('category','equalto','moe_routed')|map(attribute='bytes_per_token')|first / (w.decode_step.unique_bytes / w.decode_step.accept_len)) }} of all bytes and run at about 4 FLOP/B: each touched expert serves about one row.
- **Attention core** (one shared 512-wide latent, 64 heads) has high arithmetic intensity, because the KV is tiny: {{ si(w.decode_table|selectattr('category','equalto','attn_core')|map(attribute='bytes_per_token')|first, 'B') }} per token.
- **All-to-all vs all-reduce.** Expert parallelism moves {{ w.ep_alltoall_bytes_per_layer|si('B') }} per layer (dispatch + combine of 6 rows x 6 experts x 5,120 x BF16). The two TP all-reduces of the engine move {{ w.tp_allreduce_bytes_per_layer|si('B') }} per layer. Both are tiny. Thus, at batch 1, the cost of collectives is latency, not bandwidth.
- **Engram is a capacity problem, not a bandwidth problem.** The two tables hold {{ (w.engram.table_bytes/1e9)|n(0) }} GB. The engine shards them into GPU HBM, {{ (w.engram.table_bytes/4/1e9)|n(0) }} GB per GB300. A step looks up only {{ w.engram.lookup_bytes_per_step|si('B') }}: about {{ (w.engram.lookup_bytes_per_step / (K.meas4.step_ms/1e3) / 1e6)|n(0) }} MB/s. The token ids give the addresses before the step starts (tech report §2.4.2).

